  "strategy": "1. Approach and rationale\n   We propose a fundamentally different approach from previous optimization based methods by employing a dual-parameter geometric construction inspired by wavelet scaling. The core idea is that optimal step functions for minimizing maximum overlap exhibit a fractal-like self-similarity across scales. We represent the function using dyadic scales and optimization templates with multi-resolution properties, enabling global optimization that escapes local minima. The method forms h(x) as a weighted sum of adaptive pulse functions with scale-dependent amplitudes. This leverages the observation that signal atoms of varying widths can be jointly optimized to significantly reduce worst-case overlaps.\n\n   The algorithm uses triangle-based wavelet scaling to represent the function, which provides three key advantages: (1) coherence across scales for graceful resolution transfer, (2) adaptive amplitude adjustment via exponential weighting that encourages balanced energy distribution, and (3) built-in handle of boundary effects through localized support. This differs from standard grid discretization by working in a transformation domain with properties that suppress the combinatorial impact.\n\n   Critically, we address the boundaries with a specialized clamping mechanism and use temporal regularity constraints to preserve integral constants during hierarchical optimization.\n\n2. Mathematical specification\n   Variables:\n   - n_dyadic (int): Number of dyadic levels (e.g., 4 to 8)\n   - j in [0, n_dyadic-1] for scale levels\n   - m in [0, M_j-1] (M_j multiples per level j)\n   - \u03c6_{j,m}(x): Position of wavelet leaf (triangle function at position m * 2^(-j))\n   - w_{j,m}: Weight factor for leaf m at level j\n   - \u03b3 for global scale balance (\u22651)\n\n   Objective:\n   Minimize C_5(h) = max_k \u222b_0^2 h(x)(1-h(x+k))dx\n\n   Constraints:\n   - \u03a3_{j,m} w_{j,m} = n_points/2 exactly\n   - 0 \u2264 h(x) \u2264 1 for all x\n   - Integral constraint via sparse representation and auto-normalization\n\n   Representation:\n   h(x) = (1/2) + \u03a3_{j,m} \u03b3^{-j} \u03c6_{j,m}(x)\n   where \u03c6_{j,m}(x) = max(0, 1 - |2(x - base_{j,m})|) - clipped to [0,1]\n\n   The optimization objective is implemented via finite-differences and augmented Lagrangian techniques. Constraint handling is built-in via automatic amplitude scaling.\n\n3. Implementation procedure\n   a) Precomputation:\n      - Define dyadic grid: levels j=0 to n_dyadic-1\n      - Precompute all wavelet templates with varying support widths\n      - Build geometric mapping matrices between resolutions\n\n   b) Initialize from parent solution:\n      i. Use parent solution (if provided) and adapt to dyadic representation\n      ii. Set base weights: evenly distributed at finest level\n      iii. Compute baseline C_5 via finite-difference integration\n\n   c) Hierarchical optimization:\n      i. Coarse-to-fine traversal: start from level j=0, then j=1, etc.\n      ii. At each level, use adaptive exponential weighting (\u03b3) to:\n         balance intra-level (j) and cross-level (j+m) energies\n         lateralize overlaps (pun intended) by separating high-frequency regions\n      iii. Apply gradient-based optimization with line search for weights\n      iv. Maintain constraint satisfaction via dual projection methods\n\n   d) Energy flow control:\n      Positive feedback for promising leaves, while negative damping prevents explosion. For resolution transfers, use bilinear interpolation explicitly field samples. Cross-level equivalence is maintained via consistency constraints.\n\n   e) Termination:\n      i. Complete optimization pyramid (all levels)\n      ii. Validation via exact integral enforcement\n      iii. Local refinement through simulated annealing\n\n4. Search choices and useful variations\n   Template responses:\n   - Level optimizer: gradient-based L-BFGS with damping\n   - Topology: wavelet chirp (frequency modulation) enabled\n   - Feedback mechanism: dual exponential weighting schedule\n   - References: exploit known optimal in lower-dimensional analogs\n\n   Key adjustments:\n   - Level-dependent damping coefficient (controls exploration within level)\n   - Multi-exponential decay for amplitude contributions\n   - Window function shaping for terminal clamping\n\n   Time allocation:\n   - 40% for level-by-level optimization\n   - 30% for tree recombination and exploration\n   - 20% for boundary treatment and integral enforcement\n   - 10% for fallback if time expires\n\n5. Compute budget and robustness\n   - Level granularity: min_j = 0, max_j = n_dyadic\n   - Complexity: O(n_dyadic * M_j^2) per evaluation (M_j~O(2^j))\n   - Core timeout checks for level j: timer_level = budget_s * time_per_level\n\n   Trainer response schedule:\n   - Every 200 seconds: auto-save best candidate\n   - At level completion: regular checkpoint\n   - Fallback: revert to previously saved best candidate\n   - Dynamic stochastic adjustment under stuck convergence\n\n6. Validation and return contract\n   - Feasibility checks: integral verification \u00b11e-8, bin integrity [0,1] with tol=1e-8\n   - Objective consistency: recompute C_5 on raw samples after reconstruction\n   - Resolution transfer: accept only completed parent transfer if available\n   - Finalization: convert to binarized output for evaluation\n   - Return format: numpy array for h, final C_5 value, final n_points",
